WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.966 --> 00:00:04.347
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:08.530
NARRATOR: Girls Who Build is
an outreach effort focused

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:10.960
on getting high school
girls excited about science

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.570
and engineering.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:14.720
We hold hands on
workshops where girls

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.890
learn from industry experts and
experiment with technologies,

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:21.070
like wearable
electronics and robotics.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.170
For our latest
workshop, 40 girls

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:26.200
came to the MIT Lincoln
Laboratory Beaver Work Center

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.260
to learn all about cameras.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:32.729
We wanted to show the girls
how optics, sensors, and image

00:00:32.729 --> 00:00:34.930
processing combine
to make apps they use

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:37.220
everyday, like Instagram, work.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:40.320
GIRL: I got to see how filters
work and how they're made

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.240
and how I can make them too.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.830
NARRATOR: The girl tore apart
old digital and film cameras

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:46.290
to see how they're made.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:47.850
Then they built
their own cameras

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.240
using a Raspberry Pi
computer and camera module.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:55.120
GIRL 2: It was just cool how
we had to make the camera

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:56.870
and put the different
parts together.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.520
It was made everything
a lot more clear

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:00.616
about how normal cameras work.

00:01:00.616 --> 00:01:02.240
NARRATOR: The girls
heard from speakers

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.110
who talked about how cameras
are used in fields as diverse

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:08.540
as searching for exoplanets,
underwater salvage,

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:11.342
improving cancer treatment,
and 3D tomography.

00:01:11.342 --> 00:01:12.800
GIRL 3: My favorite
part of the day

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.174
was listening to
all the key note

00:01:14.174 --> 00:01:16.540
speakers because they were
all so passionate about what

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:17.610
their field was.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:21.570
But it all connected to one core
idea of the camera technology.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:22.330
GIRL 4: Whoa.

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:24.829
GIRL 5: I mean, you can move
all the way around here though.

00:01:24.829 --> 00:01:26.092
GIRL 4: That's so cool.

00:01:26.092 --> 00:01:27.550
KRISTEN RAILEY: I
started Girls Who

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:30.600
Build because I am a female
engineer who didn't grow up

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.420
fixing cars with my dad.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:35.930
I had no previous exposure
to power tools, circuits,

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:37.720
or programming before college.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.460
It's my vision that when
girls enter college,

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:42.200
they have the same
hands on engineering

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.080
background as their peers.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:47.490
I want them to realize they
can use power tools, code,

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.010
and solder too.

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:52.470
The issue isn't an inability
or intelligence, but just

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.600
a lack of exposure
to these things.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.420
I would like girls to
discover the amazing power

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:00.380
of engineering, even before
they apply to college.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:02.645
GIRL: I was definitely
thinking about computer science

00:02:02.645 --> 00:02:05.770
as a profession so I thought
this was a super cool way

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:08.800
to get more experience out
of that before I went off

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.430
to college.

